What people in this career actually do day-to-day: (Summarize the core tasks and responsibilities — in your own words.) 

Accountants work with financial information to make sure records are accurate and businesses are making good financial decisions. 
Depending on the position, they may prepare financial statements, review expenses, analyze financial records, prepare taxes, identify financial problems, and advise managers or clients.


Work environment: (Where do they work? Office? Field? Hospital? Remote? With people? Alone? Physical? Sedentary?) 

Most accountants work in an office or from home and spend a lot of time working on computers and financial documents. They may work individually, but they also work with clients, managers, and other accountants. 
Some accountants travel to clients' offices. Certain times of the year, especially tax season or quarterly reporting periods, can require longer hours.



Required education and training: (What degree level is typically required? Any certifications, licenses, or graduate degrees needed?) [Your response here]

A bachelor's degree in accounting or a related business field is typically required. Becoming a CPA is not required for every accounting job, but it can improve career opportunities.

