What people in this career actually do day-to-day: (Summarize the core tasks and responsibilities — in your own words.) 

Data scientists take large amounts of information and use statistics, programming, machine learning, and other analytical techniques to find useful patterns.
They clean and organize data, create models, test predictions, visualize results, and explain what those results mean to people making business decisions.

Work environment: (Where do they work? Office? Field? Hospital? Remote? With people? Alone? Physical? Sedentary?)

Data scientists usually work in office environments and spend significant amounts of time using computers. The work involves both independent technical work and communication with other employees or decision-makers.

Required education and training: (What degree level is typically required? Any certifications, licenses, or graduate degrees needed?) 

A bachelor's degree in computer science, mathematics, statistics, business, engineering, or a related field is usually required. Some employers prefer a master's or doctoral degree.

